ck went to the second line instead."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "G2 lost 7.6 points, taking Black from -10.9 to -18.6. The board shows White's wall on E2 to E5, with Black's stones on F2 and G3 split from the rest. The engine wanted B5, the left side, and this was the move where the game slipped out of reach."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "D3 was Black's worst move of the game at 8.6 points, moving the score from -14.2 to -22.8. The engine's choice was B4, and after D3 Black's stones on C3 and D3 sat in a thin row below White's C4."}, {"turn": 22, "text": "White's B3 lost 6.2 points, the 12k's worst move, and moved the score from -24.7 to -18.6. The lead was so large that the slip changed nothing; Black soon lost another 6.0 points with A2 and never closed the gap."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberty count, and count.py flood-fills regions to tally area score for the current position, with named stones treated as dead. The inline Python only appends a game log to CLAUDE.md. None of it proposes, ranks, or looks ahead at moves.", "stop": false}}